  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Elm Mushroom (Hypsizygus ulmarius) is a delicate, edible fungus characterized by its small, clustered fruiting bodies with pale coloring and thin stems. Named for its association with elm trees, this species is a wood-decomposing saprotroph found throughout New England during cooler months.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Clustered Growth",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies grow in dense clusters or shelves from a common base, rarely solitary",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap Color",
      "description": "Small caps (1-3 cm) are pale gray to whitish with a delicate, thin texture",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Gill Arrangement",
      "description": "Gills are white to pale gray and run down the stem (decurrent), crowded together",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Elm Mushrooms grow on dead or dying hardwood, particularly elm trees, but also on other deciduous wood in forests and wooded areas throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its name referencing elm trees, Hypsizygus ulmarius can fruit on various hardwoods and is commercially cultivated in Asia as an edible mushroom known as 'Enoki's cousin.'",
